The idea is to use prefix sums and, for each value, remember the smallest prefix sum seen just before an occurrence of that value.

1. Let `pre` be the sum of all elements before index `j`. The sum of `nums[i..j]` is then `prefix[j+1] - prefix[i]`.
2. Keep a dictionary `best` mapping each value `v` to the minimum `prefix[i]` over all earlier indices `i` where `nums[i] == v`.
3. At index `j`, first record `prefix[j]` for `nums[j]`, keeping the minimum. This is safe because `i == j` can never be good: it would need `|0| == k`, but `k >= 1`.
4. Then, for each target `t` in `nums[j] - k` and `nums[j] + k`, if `t` is in `best`, the candidate answer is `prefix[j+1] - best[t]`.
5. Track the maximum candidate. If no good subarray was found, return 0.

This runs in O(n) time with O(n) extra space.

