The value of a string depends only on how many times each letter appears: a letter with count c contributes c*(c-1)/2. So the positions of the replacement letters don't affect the value, only which letters we pick.

1. Count the fixed letters in s.
2. For each '?', greedily pick the letter with the smallest current count (ties go to the smallest letter) and increment its count. This keeps the counts as balanced as possible, which minimizes the sum of c*(c-1)/2. A heap of (count, letter) does this efficiently.
3. The greedy fixes the multiset of chosen letters, but not their order. Since the value doesn't depend on order, sort the chosen letters and place them into the '?' positions from left to right. This gives the lexicographically smallest string with the minimum value.

