For each ending index, there are at most about 30 distinct OR values among the subarrays that end there. This is because each value is a superset of the bits of the next shorter one, so a new value must add at least one bit.

So we keep the set of distinct ORs for subarrays ending at the previous index. For the current element `x`, the new set is `{x}` plus `x | v` for every `v` in the old set. We check every value in the new set against `k` and track the minimum absolute difference.

This runs in O(n · 30) time.

```python
from typing import List

class Solution:
    def minimumDifference(self, nums: List[int], k: int) -> int:
        best = float('inf')
        cur = set()
        for x in nums:
            cur = {x | v for v in cur}
