response_text:
Approach: a special substring must contain every occurrence of each character inside it. So for each character `c`, take the interval from the first occurrence of `c` to its last occurrence. Expand it until it is closed:

1. Scan the interval left to right.
2. If a character inside has its first occurrence before the interval start, then no valid substring can start at `first[c]`. Discard this candidate.
3. Otherwise, extend the right end to that character's last occurrence and keep scanning.

Each surviving candidate `[first[c], R]` is the minimal special substring starting at `first[c]`. Drop any candidate equal to the whole string.

There are at most 26 candidates, and each expansion costs O(n). So building them takes O(26n).

Then pick the maximum number of non-overlapping intervals with the greedy rule: sort by right endpoint and take an interval whenever it starts after the last chosen end.

Why using only these minimal intervals is enough: any special substring contains the closure of some character it holds, and that closure is itself a candidate interval. So a set of disjoint special substrings can be shrunk to a set of disjoint candidates of the same size.

