For a row permutation p and a column permutation q, the rearranged grid has A[p[i]][q[j]] in cell (i, j). The pair is valid if this equals B[i][j] for every cell.

The minimum number of adjacent swaps needed to realize a permutation equals its number of inversions. Row swaps and column swaps act independently, so the cost of a valid pair is inv(p) + inv(q). The answer is the minimum cost over all valid pairs, or -1 if no pair is valid.

