Every sequence of swaps ends up applying some row permutation p and some column permutation q to A. After that, cell (i, j) of the result holds A[p[i]][q[j]].

The cheapest way to reach a given permutation with adjacent swaps uses exactly as many swaps as the permutation has inversions. So the cost of a pair (p, q) is inv(p) + inv(q).

With H, W ≤ 5 there are at most 120 × 120 = 14400 pairs. For each pair we check the 25 cells, which is fast enough to brute force.

To prune early, for each p we first check whether row p[i] of A, sorted, matches row i of B, sorted. Even without this pruning the search is fast.

