response_text:
Two numbers multiply to a perfect square exactly when they have the same square-free part (the number left after removing every squared prime factor). So the plan is:

1. **Handle zeros separately.** Zero times anything is 0, which is a square. With z zeros among N numbers, the pairs containing at least one zero number z*(N-z) + z*(z-1)/2.
2. **Compute square-free parts.** Build a smallest-prime-factor sieve up to 2×10^5. For each nonzero value, factor it and keep only the primes that appear an odd number of times.
3. **Count matching pairs.** Group the nonzero values by square-free part. Each group of size c contributes c*(c-1)/2 pairs.

A faster alternative for step 2 is to precompute the square-free part of every value up to the maximum by dividing out each square d² in turn. That would also work, but factoring with the sieve is simpler and fast enough.

