This is a depth-first search over self-avoiding walks, started from every empty cell, with pruning of the last two levels so it runs fast enough in Python.

**Why plain DFS is feasible.** The number of self-avoiding walks of length 11 on a square lattice is about 120,000 per start cell. With at most 100 start cells, that is roughly 12 million paths in the worst case. Enumerating all of them one call at a time would be slow in Python, so the last two steps are counted instead of explored.

**Representation.**
- Each cell gets an index and a bit. `nbmask[v]` is the set of empty neighbours of `v`, stored as a bitmask.
- `vis` is the bitmask of cells already on the current path.

**Counting the last two steps.**
- **One step left:** the number of completions is the number of unvisited neighbours, `popcount(nbmask[v] & ~vis)`.
- **Two steps left:** for each unvisited neighbour `u` of `v`, add `popcount(nbmask[u] & ~vis)`.
  - `v` is already in `vis`, so it is excluded automatically.
  - `u` is never its own neighbour, so it needs no special handling.

This leaves only about 25,000 recursive calls per start cell, or about 2.5 million in total, which is fast enough.

**Answer.** Run the DFS from every empty cell with that cell marked as visited, and add up the counts.

